    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my security+ and i'm a bit stuck. if you already hash a password, why do you even need to add a salt? wouldnt the hash itself already make it unreadable? wouldn't salting just be an extra step for no reason?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right that a hash alone makes the password unreadable, but a salt helps protect against rainbow table attacks and collisions. Without salt, an attacker can pre-compute hashes of common passwords (rainbow tables) and compare them to the hash stored in a database. A salt, added to the password before hashing, ensures the rainbows are different, making these pre-computed tables useless. This helps prevent attackers from cracking, even if they obtain the original hash.",
